olved from a nematode which invades its host by penetrating the skin?(15 points) IV. List ten parasites covered this semester which are fecal/oral in their transmission. Provide one epidemiological factor which details how they have been shown to be transmitted. V. List ten helminths. Create a taxonomic key based upon the ova morphology. Hint....use size, opercula, embryonation, shell features, and internal morphology as criteria. (20 points)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"de0bf2d4170e2abda8ea4bd7dc3673879d51a095"},{"id":"q-ffddc04b682b","stem":"To combat Texas cattle fever caused by Babesia bigemina, the vector tick, Boophilus annulatus, was eradicated from the United States through an ambitious program of intensive dipping of cattle. What aspect of the life cycle of this tick allowed the eradication program to be successful?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boophilus annulatus is a one-host tick"},{"label":"B","text":"Boophilus annulatus prefers to feed on animals other than cattle"},{"label":"C","text":"Boophilus annulatus is a three-host tick"},{"label":"D","text":"Boophilus annulatus has low tolerance for conditions of high humidity"},{"label":"E","text":"Boophilus annulatus has a low tolerance for high temperatures"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9f4e859302d","stem":"Which of the following animals would be most likely to serve as a source of Trichinella spp. infection to humans in the U.S.?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"rabbit"},{"label":"B","text":"sheep"},{"label":"C","text":"cattle"},{"label":"D","text":"bear"},{"label":"E","text":"deer"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-1da182b4a583","stem":"This parasite ova was found on fecal flotation of a 5-year-old dog with a history of intermittent bloody diarrhea. Which of the following is the most appropriate labeled pharmacotherapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"pyrantel"},{"label":"B","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"C","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"D","text":"trimethoprim-sulfa"},{"label":"E","text":"fenbendazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-37c05249118b","stem":"An egg (ova) of Pearsonema plica was detected on urine sediment examination of a 4-year- old neutered male mixed-breed dog with a history of cystitis. How did the dog become infected with this parasite?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"skin penetration of third-stage larvae"},{"label":"B","text":"ingestion of earthworm containing first-stage larvae"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of egg containing third-stage larvae"},{"label":"D","text":"migration of third-stage larvae up urethra to embed in urinary bladder mucosa"},{"label":"E","text":"ingestion of dung beetle containing third-stage larvae"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39da842ca775","stem":"A 5-year-old gelding presents with ataxia and incoordination in all four limbs. A CSF tap reveals antibodies to Sarcocystis neurona. How did the horse most likely acquire this infection?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"transmission of sporozoites via the bite of a tabanid fly"},{"label":"B","text":"ingestion of oocysts from the feces of an infected horse"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of sarcocysts in the muscle tissue of an infected opossum"},{"label":"D","text":"transmission of sporozoites via tick bite"},{"label":"E","text":"ingestion of sporocysts from opossum feces in contaminated feed or water"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3399f56a49a1","stem":"This parasite was detected on fecal flotation of a 3-month-old puppy with acute diarrhea. Although many dogs will self-cure this parasite, you decide to prescribe a treatment. What treatment is most appropriate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"sulfadimethoxine"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"D","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"E","text":"pyrantel"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ef1112c94c01","stem":"An adult budgie presents with severe crusting on the beak and cere which has resulted in severe deformity of the beak. What diagnostic assay is most likely to reveal the etiologic agent?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"serum chemistry profile"},{"label":"B","text":"bacterial culture of the crusted material for the presence of Staphylococcus"},{"label":"C","text":"microscopic examination of the crusted material for mites"},{"label":"D","text":"radiographs to evaluate calcium deposition in the long bones"},{"label":"E","text":"virus isolation attempts on the crusted material"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d46ca24d3b7","stem":"West Nile virus is commonly transmitted to horses, people, and other animals via","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"fleas"},{"label":"B","text":"mosquitoes"},{"label":"C","text":"horse flies"},{"label":"D","text":"ticks"},{"label":"E","text":"biting midges"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00ad54f63137","stem":"Which of the following groups of ectoparasites is not known to occur on pet birds?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"sucking lice (Anoplura)"},{"label":"B","text":"chewing lice (Mallophaga)"},{"label":"C","text":"fleas (Siphonaptera)"},{"label":"D","text":"mites (Acari)"},{"label":"E","text":"all of these groups of parasites commonly occur on pet birds"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-b1c79a460370","stem":"An intact male feral kitten presents for routine examination. You notice large numbers of glistening white eggs adherent to the hair shafts on the dorsal and lateral aspect of the thorax. Closer inspection reveals several dorsoventrally flattened insects clinging to the hair shafts themselves. What parasite is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phthirus"},{"label":"B","text":"Linognathus"},{"label":"C","text":"Hematopinus"},{"label":"D","text":"Pediculus"},{"label":"E","text":"Felicola"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1393f58cebac","stem":"This egg was detected on flotation of feces from a 3-year- old barn cat. How did the cat become infected with this parasite?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ingestion of infected flea"},{"label":"B","text":"ingestion of infected rodent"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of infected rabbit"},{"label":"D","text":"transmitted via bite of infected flea"},{"label":"E","text":"transmitted via bite of infected rodent Page 2 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6ab2df7a5cad","stem":"A 5-month-old intact female barn kitten presents with a subcutaneous mass in her neck. Upon examination you notice an opening in the mass. This organism was removed with forceps. What parasite is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oestrus"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoderma"},{"label":"C","text":"Gasterophilus"},{"label":"D","text":"Cuterebra"},{"label":"E","text":"Cephenmyia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-adeb627ac0b7","stem":"Which of the following are effective for the treatment of clinical coccidiosis in cattle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"amprolium"},{"label":"B","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"C","text":"decoquinate"},{"label":"D","text":"a and b"},{"label":"E","text":"all three drugs listed"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ef59959c37c","stem":"Pigs become infected with the thorny-headed worm, Macracanthorhynchus hirudinaceus, upon","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ingestion of free L3 from the environment"},{"label":"B","text":"skin penetration by L3 from the environment"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of L3 in larvated eggs from the environment"},{"label":"D","text":"ingestion of an infected beetle containing a cystacanth stage"},{"label":"E","text":"ingestion of L1 in the fresh feces of a fellow pig"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c1798198d830","stem":"Lice infestations on cattle are more common in the","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"winter"},{"label":"B","text":"spring"},{"label":"C","text":"summer"},{"label":"D","text":"fall"},{"label":"E","text":"there is no seasonal pattern to louse infestation"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f25dcab3c79e","stem":"Cats infected with Dirofilaria immitis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"routinely develop severe reactions to administration of monthly heartworm preventatives due to rapid death of microfilaria"},{"label":"B","text":"are usually microfilaremic, allowing diagnosis via Knott or filter test or examination of a wet mount of whole blood"},{"label":"C","text":"occasionally develop disease due to aberrant migration of the worms, including to the central nervous system or ocular tissues"},{"label":"D","text":"often harbor large numbers (>50) of adult worms"},{"label":"E","text":"have not been shown to develop any significant clinical disease due to infection"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-212bbcbff8c5","stem":"Which of the following drugs is effective for treating Giardia and has the least toxicity problems associated with its use in dogs and cats?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"B","text":"milbemycin"},{"label":"C","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"D","text":"albendazole"},{"label":"E","text":"fenbendazole Page 3 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-dce85e327f73","stem":"Fecal flotation on a 4-year-old intact male cat reveals this parasite ova. What clinical disease is this parasite most commonly associated with?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cystitis"},{"label":"B","text":"neurologic disease (circling, ataxia)"},{"label":"C","text":"respiratory disease"},{"label":"D","text":"vomiting"},{"label":"E","text":"anemia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-68227a783874","stem":"A rabbit with a severe ear infection caused by mites is brought to your clinic. The organism most likely responsible is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psoroptes cuniculi"},{"label":"B","text":"Otodectes cynotis"},{"label":"C","text":"Knemidocoptes mutans"},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcoptes scabiei"},{"label":"E","text":"Polyplax serrata"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4afa4bc58ac","stem":"Anthelmintic resistance has been widely documented to occur in small strongyles (= cyathostomes) of horses with which of the following dewormers?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"pyrantel"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"D","text":"moxidectin"},{"label":"E","text":"a and b"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b83dbf83b28a","stem":"What arthropods are commonly known to transmit Anaplasma marginale between cattle in the United States?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermacentor ticks"},{"label":"B","text":"Tabanus horse flies"},{"label":"C","text":"biting midges (Culicoides)"},{"label":"D","text":"mosquitoes"},{"label":"E","text":"a and b"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4075c7de0a2f","stem":"A dog presents with severe pruritus. Alopecia, erythematous skin, and hyperkeratosis are evident on the lateral elbows, lateral hocks, and the margins of the pinna. The dog scratches himself repeatedly during your examination, but otherwise appears healthy. What diagnostic test should you perform first?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"skin scrape"},{"label":"B","text":"fecal flotation"},{"label":"C","text":"fecal sedimentation"},{"label":"D","text":"urinalysis with sediment examination"},{"label":"E","text":"complete blood count and serum chemistry panel"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c21f68468231","stem":"In recent years, several commercially-available equine dewormers have been modified to include praziquantel. What was responsible for this change?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"new data demonstrating the clinical association between trematodes and colic"},{"label":"B","text":"new data demonstrating the clinical association between tapeworms and colic"},{"label":"C","text":"new data describing trematodes as a parasite of horses"},{"label":"D","text":"new data describing tapeworms as a parasite of horses"},{"label":"E","text":"recognition of widespread anthelmintic resistance in equine nematodes to the avermectin dewormers (ivermectin, moxidectin, etc...) Page 4 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-0dd6b97e2084","stem":"A litter of 2-week-old kittens presents covered with a huge number of fleas. The kittens are hypothermic, have pale mucous membranes, and appear moribund. Which of the following would not be an appropriate means of managing these kittens","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"apply high concentration spot on pyrethrin (45-65%) residual flea control product to each kitten"},{"label":"B","text":"keep the kittens warm by placing on a circulating-water heating pad"},{"label":"C","text":"check PCV and consider blood transfusion if indicated"},{"label":"D","text":"immediately remove fleas via flea comb and gentle shampoo"},{"label":"E","text":"administer supplemental iron and vitamin B12 to each kitten"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f08f43bee211","stem":"A client brought in these nematodes after his 6-year-old spayed female indoor/outdoor cat vomited them onto the kitchen floor this morning. How did the cat most likely become infected?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"transplacental transmission from the queen as a kitten"},{"label":"B","text":"transmammary transmission from the queen as a kitten"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of an insect intermediate host or vertebrate paratenic host"},{"label":"D","text":"ingestion of larvated egg from the enviroment"},{"label":"E","text":"ingestion of the third-stage larvae from the vomit of another cat"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bf66a55bee85","stem":"Dogs acquire infection with Hepatozoon sp. upon","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"tick bite"},{"label":"B","text":"flea bite"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of infected ticks"},{"label":"D","text":"ingestion of infected fleas"},{"label":"E","text":"none of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4e8cc499cca3","stem":"A cat is infested with mites that you identify as Dermanyssus species. You ask the client","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whether or not the cat has access to birds"},{"label":"B","text":"Whether or not the cat is a mouse hunter"},{"label":"C","text":"Whether or not the cat spends a lot of time near rabbit dens"},{"label":"D","text":"Whether or not the family dog has been pruritic"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a0da2b246ca","stem":"The term “measly beef” refers to the presence of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"eggs of Taenia saginata in muscle tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"cysticerci of Taenia saginata in muscle tissue"},{"label":"C","text":"sarcocysts (tissue cysts) of Sarcocystis in muscle tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"tissue cysts of Toxoplasma gondii in muscle tissue"},{"label":"E","text":"tissue cysts of Neospora caninum in muscle tissue"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ab5b3a0ee13","stem":"Neospora caninum infection in cattle causes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"weight loss"},{"label":"B","text":"bloat"},{"label":"C","text":"sterility in bulls"},{"label":"D","text":"abortion"},{"label":"E","text":"chorioretinitis in aged (> 10 years) animals Page 5 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2b3ce07db6f9","stem":"Infection with which of the following parasites has been associated with head tilt and ataxia in rabbits?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Encephalitozoon cuniculi"},{"label":"B","text":"Baylisascaris procyonis"},{"label":"C","text":"Obeliscoides cuniculi"},{"label":"D","text":"Passalurus ambiguous"},{"label":"E","text":"a and b"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5eeaa5cd4f2c","stem":"A 2-year-old indoor/outdoor neutered male cat presents with lethargy, depression, and anorexia of two days duration. Physical examination reveals a profoundly icteric, moribund cat with pale mucous membranes and severe dehydration. These organisms were found on blood smear. As you explain your treatment plan and prognosis to the client, she asks if there is anything she can do to protect her four other cats at home from becoming infected. What do you recommend?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"prevent ingestion of rodents and rabbits"},{"label":"B","text":"flea control, including keeping cats indoors and on highly effective flea control products"},{"label":"C","text":"tick control, including keeping cats indoors and on highly effective tick control products"},{"label":"D","text":"prevent ingestion of birds"},{"label":"E","text":"administer a monthly heartworm preventative with intestinal parasite control"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-de1692a5b69f","stem":"Control of Potomac horse fever in unvaccinated horses includes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"administering prophylactic doses of praziquantel to control the vector flukes"},{"label":"B","text":"routine application of insecticides to horses to prevent feeding by flies"},{"label":"C","text":"administering prophylactic doses of penicillin antibiotics to control the Neorickettsia (= Ehrlichia) risticii"},{"label":"D","text":"avoiding aquatic habitats that may be more likely to harbor infected trematodes"},{"label":"E","text":"protecting feed and water from possible contamination with opossum feces"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7830fe12e4da","stem":"A five-year-old horse presents with tail rubbing and anal pruritus. You suspect infection with the equine pinworm, Oxyuris equi. What diagnostic test is preferred to recover the eggs of this parasite?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"direct smear"},{"label":"B","text":"passive fecal flotation with sodium nitrate"},{"label":"C","text":"centrifugal fecal flotation with sugar solution"},{"label":"D","text":"fecal sedimentation"},{"label":"E","text":"scotch tape test"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-869746b9071b","stem":"Treatment of dogs with severe, generalized demodectic mange should always include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"antibiotics"},{"label":"B","text":"anti-mite agents"},{"label":"C","text":"anti-fungal agents"},{"label":"D","text":"antibiotics and anti-mite agents"},{"label":"E","text":"anti-fungal agents and anti-mite agents Page 6 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c07ded547952","stem":"A litter of 2-week-old puppies presents with acute lethargy. On physical examination the puppies are moribund, cold, and have markedly pale mucous membranes. You suspect acute anemia due to hookworm infection. The next step in managing these puppies is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"prophylactically deworm with pyrantel pamoate"},{"label":"B","text":"perform a fecal flotation and only deworm if eggs are detected"},{"label":"C","text":"perform CBC, serum chemsitry, U/A, and abdominal radiographs on each pup"},{"label":"D","text":"hold for quiet observation for 48 hours to monitor progression of disease"},{"label":"E","text":"bathe each puppy to insure ectoparasites are eliminated"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5b7b0c9fd8a8","stem":"These eggs were found on fecal flotation from a 10-year-old spayed female indoor/outdoor cat. Is this an unusual diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"no, this parasite is commonly seen in both kittens and adult cats"},{"label":"B","text":"yes, this parasite is extremely rare in cats in the United States"},{"label":"C","text":"yes, this cat is likely immunocompromised and thus the immunity that would prevent the infection has lapsed"},{"label":"D","text":"yes, this parasite is generally only found in kittens"},{"label":"E","text":"no, this parasite is commonly seen in the feces of coprophagic cats that ingest the eggs in the feces of another animal"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4aeb10f6b9eb","stem":"What aspect of the life cycle of Habronema and Draschia spp. accounts for the seasonal pattern seen in cutaneous habronemiasis, or summer sores, in affected horses?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the bacterial organisms which cause the secondary infections responsible for the lesions only flourish in the humid summer months"},{"label":"B","text":"the larval stages of these nematodes become hypobiotic in the winter and are thus less irritating to horses"},{"label":"C","text":"the ticks which transmit these parasites are only active in the summer months"},{"label":"D","text":"the activity of the muscid flies which serve as vector and intermediate host of these nematodes is greatly reduced in the winter months"},{"label":"E","text":"horses are ridden less in the winter months and thus the abrasive sores are less likely to develop"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-22bd7055bbed","stem":"You examine an adult Himalayan cat who has just returned from Panama with his owner. The cat has a persistent, draining wound on the right lateral flank. You sedate the cat to clip the hair and examine the wound and discover several fly larvae, each ~ 1 cm long and bearing two distinct pigmented tracheal trunks. What parasite is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calliphora vomitoria"},{"label":"B","text":"Haematobia irritans"},{"label":"C","text":"Felicola subrostratus"},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcophaga carnaria"},{"label":"E","text":"Cochliomyia hominovorax"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bd97e99e4567","stem":"Which of the following antiparasitic agents is most suitable for use against immature stages of Fasciola hepatica?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"B","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"C","text":"praziquantel"},{"label":"D","text":"albendazole"},{"label":"E","text":"clorsulon Page 7 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-72f6b0b70b09","stem":"A 4-year-old neutered male outdoor cat from Florida presents with a 2-week history of weight loss, vomiting, and diarrhea. This parasite was produced in the vomitus. What is the most appropriate treatment?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"selamectin"},{"label":"D","text":"praziquantel"},{"label":"E","text":"milbemycin oxime"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1cd1d878e229","stem":"An 8-year-old mixed breed mare presents with head shaking and aural irritation due to the presence of several heavily-spined nymphal soft ticks in both ears. What parasite is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ixodes"},{"label":"B","text":"Dermacentor"},{"label":"C","text":"Otobius"},{"label":"D","text":"Amblyomma"},{"label":"E","text":"Rhipicephalus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3e749b6d4100","stem":"The short prepatent period of this nematode insures that eggs of this parasite are likely to be the first detected in a fecal float on a young foal","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxyuris equi"},{"label":"B","text":"Strongylus vulgaris"},{"label":"C","text":"Parascaris equorum"},{"label":"D","text":"Strongyloides westeri"},{"label":"E","text":"Drashia megastoma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5ccd4781d18","stem":"A mixed breed dog that is housed outdoors on soil in a run presents with lesions on its ventral and dorsal surfaces of all four feet, sternum, and ventral abdomen. The areas are erythematous, thickened, and alopecic. The owner informs you that ever since the dog was adopted two years ago from a neighbor, the dog has only had yearly routine vaccinations (rabies, DA2PP). The organism responsible for these lesions is most likely","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aelurostrongylus abstrusus"},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostoma tubaeforme"},{"label":"C","text":"Protostrongylus rufescens"},{"label":"D","text":"Ancylostoma caninum"},{"label":"E","text":"Bunostomum phlebotomum"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0ec144b80cf","stem":"A client has just returned from a winter vacation to Greece where she adopted a stray dog. She is concerned about the multiple patches of alopecia on the dog’s face and pinnae; you also note that the nasal planum is depigmented. With leishmaniasis as your top differential diagnosis, which statement is the least accurate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The organism is found intracellularly in erythrocytes."},{"label":"B","text":"It is unknown exactly how infection is spread from dog to dog within North America."},{"label":"C","text":"This disease is only very rarely reported in cats."},{"label":"D","text":"Horses can also suffer from the cutaneous form."},{"label":"E","text":"The incubation period can be as long as several years. Page 8 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Psychiatry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c22c12903da1","stem":"week-old kitten with a 3-day history of external otitis and aural pruritus. Of the choices listed, what is the most appropriate therapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an organophosphate/carbamate (e.g. fenthion, carbaryl)"},{"label":"B","text":"a benzimidazole (e.g. fenbendazole)"},{"label":"C","text":"an avermectin/milbemycin (e.g. ivermectin, milbemycin oxime, selamectin)"},{"label":"D","text":"amoxicillin"},{"label":"E","text":"doxycycline"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-877cb43995ca","stem":"A 5-year-old intact male cat present with choleostasis and liver failure. Fecal sedimentation reveals an operculated egg with a fully-formed miracidium. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasciola hepatica"},{"label":"B","text":"Platynosomum fastosum"},{"label":"C","text":"Taenia taeniaeformis"},{"label":"D","text":"Spirometra mansonoides"},{"label":"E","text":"Dipylidium caninum"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bf60aa9e8e9c","stem":"This parasite is unusual among the trichostrongyles in that it not only infects ruminants, but also infects many other host species including horses and pigs. It is one of the few parasites of potential concern in a mixed-species grazing situation.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cooperia punctata"},{"label":"B","text":"Trichostrongylus axei"},{"label":"C","text":"Haemonchus contortus"},{"label":"D","text":"Nematodirus battus"},{"label":"E","text":"Ostertagia ostertagi"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a53772a88aad","stem":"Foals become infected with Parascaris equorum","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"when they ingest larvated eggs from a contaminated environment"},{"label":"B","text":"transplacentally (in utero) from the somatic tissue stores in the mare"},{"label":"C","text":"transmammarily (via milk) from larvae mobilized from mare somatic tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"by inadvertent ingestion of insect intermediate hosts when grazing"},{"label":"E","text":"by inadvertent ingestion of avian tissues in contaminated feed"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1656f3f8ab2e","stem":"The main significance of Acanthocheilonema (=Dipetalonema) reconditum in veterinary medicine is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"severe disease caused by death of microfilaria following treatment for Dirofilaria immitis"},{"label":"B","text":"pathology seen in infected fleas and future potential for biological flea control"},{"label":"C","text":"severe pathology caused by migration of this subcutaneous worm"},{"label":"D","text":"microfilaria are similar in appearance to those of Dirofilaria immitis and thus may confound diagnosis"},{"label":"E","text":"severe disease caused by death of subcutaneous adult nematodes following treatment for Dirofilaria immitis Page 9 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1c1a10f26579","stem":"Routine fecal flotation from a 4-year-old cat with no history of clinical disease revealed this parasite egg. Adults of this parasite are most likely to be found in the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"esophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"nasal, tracheal, and bronchial mucosa"},{"label":"C","text":"large intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"stomach"},{"label":"E","text":"lung parenchyma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-74b20233ca3d","stem":"Although now rarely seen in managed horses, the migrating larvae of this large strongyle can cause severe inflammation in the wall of the cranial mesenteric artery, and this nematode was, at one time, the most commonly diagnosed cause of equine colic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Cranial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongylus minor"},{"label":"B","text":"Strongylus edentatus"},{"label":"C","text":"Strongylus equinus"},{"label":"D","text":"Strongylus cyathostomus"},{"label":"E","text":"Strongylus vulgaris"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8b7a594628a","stem":"You perform rectal palpation on a 2-year-old standardbred horse with a history of acute diarrhea and colic. When you withdraw your arm you notice several bright red larval worms wriggling around on the palpation sleeve. What equine nematodes are most likely responsible?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ascarids (Parascaris equorum)"},{"label":"B","text":"large strongyles (Strongylus spp)"},{"label":"C","text":"small strongyles (cyathostomes)"},{"label":"D","text":"pinworms (Oxyuris equi)"},{"label":"E","text":"threadworms (Strongyloides westeri)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e75fd4d5d8ed","stem":"A 5-month-old spaniel puppy vomits a mass of thick stout white nematodes. On close inspection, you note the presence of three lips at the anterior end of each of the worms. What parasite is this most likely to be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxocara canis"},{"label":"B","text":"Toxocara cati"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxascaris leonina"},{"label":"D","text":"Ancylostoma caninum"},{"label":"E","text":"Trichuris vulpis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da6978006420","stem":"Which of the following nematodes is free-living by nature, but occasionally causes opportunistic infections associated with dermatitis in animals?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhabditis"},{"label":"B","text":"Physaloptera"},{"label":"C","text":"Gongylonema"},{"label":"D","text":"Toxascaris"},{"label":"E","text":"Dioctophyme"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-daaa01a256c6","stem":"A dog that chases and eats wild rabbits is most likely to become infected with which cestode?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Echinococcus granulosus"},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia saginata"},{"label":"C","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},{"label":"D","text":"Dipylidium caninum"},{"label":"E","text":"Taenia pisiformis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"A dog that chases and eats wild rabbits is most likely to become infected with which cestode?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"Echinococcus granulosus"},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia saginata"},{"label":"C","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},{"label":"D","text":"Dipylidium caninum"},{"label":"E","text":"Taenia pisiformis Page 10 of 19"}]}},{"id":"q-825d61aa1240","stem":"A pigeon presents with a 1-week history of anorexia and weight loss. Accumulations of a white, caseous exudate are present in the oral cavity. A wet mount of this material reveals several pear-shaped flagellated protozoa with an undulating membrane. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichinella"},{"label":"B","text":"Trichosomoides"},{"label":"C","text":"Trichostrongylus"},{"label":"D","text":"Trichomonas"},{"label":"E","text":"Trypanosoma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-25d66b7edc6a","stem":"In recent years, several outbreaks of trichinosis in humans have been associated with ingestion of undercooked meat from horses containing infective larvae of Trichinella spiralis. The Trichinella infection in the horses likely resulted from unusual management practices prior to market; horses are not considered a likely natural host for Trichinella spp. because they are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"not arctic in origin"},{"label":"B","text":"not marsupials"},{"label":"C","text":"mammals"},{"label":"D","text":"monogastric"},{"label":"E","text":"herbivores"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-97fa370a84be","stem":"This parasite was found on fecal flotation from a newly-acquired kitten with diarrhea. After you present your treatment plan, the clients ask if their dogs are also at risk of developing disease due to infection with this parasite. You respond","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"no, this parasite is species specific and only infects cats"},{"label":"B","text":"yes, but because this parasite requires an intermediate host to develop to the infectious stage, direct transmission from the kitten to the dogs is unlikely"},{"label":"C","text":"yes, this parasite transmits freely between dogs and cats"},{"label":"D","text":"as long as the dogs are on a monthly heartworm preventative that also controls intestinal parasites they should be safe from infection"},{"label":"E","text":"as long as they prevent the dogs from ingesting cat feces from the litter box the dogs should be safe from infection"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f3c08d4fadc","stem":"Differentiation between a tick larva and a tick nymph is best achieved how?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A tick larva has eight legs and a nymph has six."},{"label":"B","text":"A tick larva has six legs and a nymph has eight."},{"label":"C","text":"The chelicerae are not present on a tick larva."},{"label":"D","text":"A tick larva is only found in the environment and never on an animal."},{"label":"E","text":"A tick larva has only an anal pore while both an anal and genital pore are present in a nymph."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c106b945a0fe","stem":"Which of the following drugs would be the best choice for the treatment of a Fasciola hepatica infection in a pregnant beef cow?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"albendazole"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"eprinomectin"},{"label":"D","text":"clorsulon"},{"label":"E","text":"praziquantel Page 11 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a830a3650a36","stem":"An 8-year-old dairy cow presents with nodular lesions on her neck from which a caseous material is expressed. Microscopic examination of the material reveals these organisms. You recommend","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"no treatment because this is a self-limiting and non- contagious condition"},{"label":"B","text":"isolate the affected cow and immediately report to state and federal veterinarians"},{"label":"C","text":"cull the affected cow to prevent spread through the herd"},{"label":"D","text":"treat with a single application of a pour-on avermectin"},{"label":"E","text":"aggressively treat every 2 weeks for several months to prevent spread through the herd"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6f11332f5b86","stem":"Which of the following helminths is rare in cats in the continental United States","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipylidium caninum"},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostoma tubaeforme"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxocara cati"},{"label":"D","text":"Trichuris felis"},{"label":"E","text":"Taenia taeniaeformis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb315831b418","stem":"You examine a pruritic pig with crusting hyperkeratotic lesions on the pinna of both ears. You confirm your intial diagnosis with a microscopic examination of a skin scrape. What treatment is most likely to be effective?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"lincomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"D","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"E","text":"toltrazuril"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88c5ca3edebf","stem":"A five-year old intact female Labrador retriever presents for routine spay. At laparotomy, a stout red nematode nearly 1 meter in length is found free in the abdominal cavity. What parasite is this?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dioctophyme renale"},{"label":"B","text":"Dipylidium caninum"},{"label":"C","text":"Dipetalonema reconditum"},{"label":"D","text":"Dirofilaria immitis"},{"label":"E","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-69279b3e31aa","stem":"Which of the following types of adult cattle are, as a group, most likely to be infected with the causative agent of bovine trichomoniasis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bulls > 4 years of age"},{"label":"B","text":"cows"},{"label":"C","text":"steers"},{"label":"D","text":"virgin heifers"},{"label":"E","text":"a and b Page 12 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-204f7020071f","stem":"Horse bot flies (Gasterophilus spp.) adhere their eggs to the hair shafts of horses. Where in the horse are the larvae of Gasterophilus spp. found?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"nasal cavity"},{"label":"B","text":"ileocecal junction"},{"label":"C","text":"stomach"},{"label":"D","text":"subcutaneous cysts on the dorsum"},{"label":"E","text":"subcutaneous cysts on the vental aspect of the neck"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b4e3b416129","stem":"A 9-year-old spayed female indoor-only cat is brought to your clinic because the client is 4 months pregnant and concerned about toxoplasmosis. Which of the following is not an appropriate course of action","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"submit a serum sample from the cat for a Toxoplasma gondii titer and recommend euthanizing the cat if the titer is positive"},{"label":"B","text":"instruct the client to only eat thoroughly cooked meat"},{"label":"C","text":"encourage the client to continue to keep the cat indoors and prevent the cat from ingesting any wildlife that may serve as reservoir hosts"},{"label":"D","text":"instruct the client that she should not feed raw meat to her cat"},{"label":"E","text":"encourage the client to have someone else change the litterbox for the duration of her pregnancy"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af8d6dd14d1f","stem":"Which organ is most commonly affected in cats that develop disease due to infection with Dirofilaria immitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"liver"},{"label":"B","text":"lung"},{"label":"C","text":"kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"central nervous system"},{"label":"E","text":"skeletal muscle"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e2dcffc62faa","stem":"Disease caused by cyathostomes, or small strongyles, in horses generally results from","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"larval irritation in the skin resulting in seasonal dermatitis, commonly referred to as “summer sores”"},{"label":"B","text":"extensive migration of larvae throughout the liver, lungs, and kidneys of infected horses"},{"label":"C","text":"aggressive blood feeding activity of the adult worms"},{"label":"D","text":"tracheal and bronchial irritation due to presence of adult worms in the airways"},{"label":"E","text":"emergence of encysted larvae from the intestinal mucosa"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cc6ce389f341","stem":"What diagnostic test is best able to detect eggs in the feces of a 1-week-old puppy infected with ascarids and hookworms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"sodium nitrate fecal flotation"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc sulfate fecal flotation with centrifugation"},{"label":"C","text":"direct smear"},{"label":"D","text":"fecal sedimentation"},{"label":"E","text":"none of these methods because eggs will not be present"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a9842e25477","stem":"Toxoplasma gondii can be transmitted to people via","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"casual contact with an infected intermediate host"},{"label":"B","text":"casual contact with a cat"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of undercooked meat"},{"label":"D","text":"ingestion of unwashed fruits and vegetables"},{"label":"E","text":"c and d Page 13 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-82c57920e2f5","stem":"Puppies that die at 2 days of age because of large numbers of larval nematodes migrating and molting in their lungs are most likely infected with which parasite?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxascaris leonina"},{"label":"B","text":"Trichuris vulpis"},{"label":"C","text":"Trichostrongylus axei"},{"label":"D","text":"Toxocara canis"},{"label":"E","text":"Physaloptera"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-34cf87d38525","stem":"Moneizia spp. are not known to cause any disease or production loss in infected cattle. However, if treatment is desired, which of the following drugs would be expected to remove these parasites from infected cattle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"B","text":"eprinomectin"},{"label":"C","text":"doramectin"},{"label":"D","text":"albendazole"},{"label":"E","text":"dichlorvos"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-072a6471d650","stem":"The currently-available monthly heartworm preventatives protect dogs from acquiring infection with adult heartworms by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"killing any larvae that may have been acquired by the dog in the preceding 30-day period"},{"label":"B","text":"providing complete protection against any newly-introduced larval worms for more than 30 days following treatment"},{"label":"C","text":"rendering the adult worms sterile, thereby preventing production of additional worms in an infected dog"},{"label":"D","text":"altering the pheromone profile of treated dogs thereby discouraging feeding by vector mosquitoes"},{"label":"E","text":"serving as a microfilaricide, thereby eliminating young worms before they are able to develop to adults in a treated dog"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2b48f784714b","stem":"You examine a herd of longhorn cattle in southern Texas with skin lesions. Affected animals have profound hyperkeratotic lesions on their neck, brisket, and ventral abdomen and have lost weight and condition since this dermatitis developed. A skin scrape reveals Sarcoptes scabiei var bovis. What is the appropriate way to manage these cows?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"immediately report your findings to appropriate state and federal authorities"},{"label":"B","text":"no treatment is needed since this is a self-limiting condition and the animals will develop immunity and self-cure"},{"label":"C","text":"treat the affected animals with a single application of a pour-on avermectin"},{"label":"D","text":"treat the affected animals with a single application of a pyrethrin/pyrethroid"},{"label":"E","text":"treat the affected animals with a single application of an organophosphate"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe5d374d040e","stem":"Which of the following biting flies have been implicated as a biological vector of African horse sickness?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles"},{"label":"B","text":"Culex"},{"label":"C","text":"Culicoides"},{"label":"D","text":"Aedes"},{"label":"E","text":"Musca Page 14 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-51bf1a9f2a97","stem":"A four-year-old mixed breed dog housed in a small, dirt-floored pen presents with a history of weight loss, diarrhea, and tenesmus. The feces is soft and contains bright red blood. What parasite is most likely to cause the symptoms described?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipylidium caninum"},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostoma caninum"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxocara canis"},{"label":"D","text":"Trichuris vulpis"},{"label":"E","text":"Taenia pisiformis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-26c4291d15fa","stem":"Neurocystocercosis develops in people following ingestion of eggs of Taenia solium found in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"undercooked meat of infected pigs"},{"label":"B","text":"feces of infected pigs"},{"label":"C","text":"feces of infected cows"},{"label":"D","text":"feces of infected people"},{"label":"E","text":"undercooked meat from infected cows"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5aa22301609a","stem":"A client who just recently established a dairy herd is concerned about the high incidence of pinkeye among her cows. You tell her that the insect that transmits the infectious agent, Moraxella bovis, is Musca autumnalis. Which of the following is not true about Musca autumnalis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adult Musca autumnalis are similar in general appearance to house flies (Musca domestica)."},{"label":"B","text":"The adult feeds on tears, saliva, nasal mucus, and blood."},{"label":"C","text":"Numbers can be controlled by piling manure into heaps to increase fermentation."},{"label":"D","text":"Permethrins can be used topically for control."},{"label":"E","text":"The eggs are laid on the cow’s face along the eyelid margin."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a4597ef3df0c","stem":"The most complete medical treatment for a cat infected with Dipylidium caninum is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"praziquantel, imidacloprid"},{"label":"B","text":"praziquantel, pyrantel"},{"label":"C","text":"pyrantel, fenbendazole"},{"label":"D","text":"pyrantel, imidacloprid"},{"label":"E","text":"fipronil, ivermectin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89f6420975d2","stem":"Which of the following products is appropriate pharmacotherapy for equine protozoal myeloencephalitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"B","text":"nitazoxanide"},{"label":"C","text":"ponazuril"},{"label":"D","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"E","text":"b and c"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e757ccd04cf8","stem":"A 3-year-old intact male terrier presents with pale mucous membranes and lethargy. Blood smear reveals large, paired piroplasms within erythrocytes. What is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"enrofloxacin"},{"label":"B","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"C","text":"sulfadimethoxine"},{"label":"D","text":"imidocarb"},{"label":"E","text":"amoxicillin Page 15 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8789df04f853","stem":"Ostertagia ostertagi is the most important nematode parasite of cattle. This nematode causes disease in cattle primarily by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"blood loss from wounds in the abomasum left by feeding adult worms"},{"label":"B","text":"destruction of the abomasal glands due to larval growth within these glands"},{"label":"C","text":"intestinal inflammation resulting from secretion of toxins from adult worms"},{"label":"D","text":"destruction of the liver, spleen, and sometimes kidneys as larvae migrate through the body before returning to the abomasum to complete their development"},{"label":"E","text":"destruction of the small intestinal mucosa, particularly in the duodenum, due to extensive larval migration in the mucosa"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6c677b3e2bb3","stem":"A 5-year-old neutered male golden retriever from Oregon with a history of salmon consumption presents with high fever, lethargy, diarrhea, and generalized lymphadenopathy. Fecal sedimentation examination reveals small operculated eggs. What is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"B","text":"doramectin"},{"label":"C","text":"doxycycline"},{"label":"D","text":"pyrantel"},{"label":"E","text":"lincomycin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f25b2b24f518","stem":"Neonatal porcine diarrhea develops subsequent to infection with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isospora suis"},{"label":"B","text":"Eimeria porci"},{"label":"C","text":"Eimeria scabra"},{"label":"D","text":"Macracanthorhynchus hirudinaceus"},{"label":"E","text":"Stephanurus dentatus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f0189492dc6","stem":"Thelazia, an eyeworm of cattle and horses that is occasionally found in small animals, is transmitted via","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Ophthalmology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"mosquitoes"},{"label":"B","text":"sandflies"},{"label":"C","text":"ticks"},{"label":"D","text":"keds"},{"label":"E","text":"muscid flies"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Ophthalmology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-83418052b840","stem":"Dogs acquire infection with Sarcocystis sp. via","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ingestion of sporulated oocyst from the environment"},{"label":"B","text":"ingestion of infected tick"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of intermediate host containing sarcocyst in muscle"},{"label":"D","text":"ingestion of naked sporocyst from the environment"},{"label":"E","text":"tick bite"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-664fb7502f80","stem":"Lesions due to infection with Ascaris suum include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bilateral ocular opacities"},{"label":"B","text":"fistulous tracts on the skin overlying the tail head"},{"label":"C","text":"milk spot lesions in the liver due to scarring"},{"label":"D","text":"laryngeal scarring causing abnormal vocalizations"},{"label":"E","text":"grossly thickened renal capsule and necrosis of perirenal fat Page 16 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-154a0c26fb3d","stem":"Flea allergy dermatitis in dogs characteristically appears","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"in the webbed area between the first and second toe"},{"label":"B","text":"in the axillary region"},{"label":"C","text":"around the eyes and mouth"},{"label":"D","text":"on the internal surface of the pinna"},{"label":"E","text":"at the base of the tail"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe63d62e2e43","stem":"You examine a herd of beef cattle with an afebrile respiratory disease that has not responded to antibiotics. Several of the younger animals are coughing. You suspect infection with a respiratory parasite. What diagnostic test would be most likely to reveal evidence of infection with the most likely parasitic etiologic agent?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"direct smear for motile protozoa"},{"label":"B","text":"blood antigen test"},{"label":"C","text":"Baermann exam for larvae in feces"},{"label":"D","text":"fecal float for ascarid eggs"},{"label":"E","text":"fecal sedimentation for trematode eggs"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-01db6ec31518","stem":"Infection of sheep and goats with Bunostomum would be expected to cause","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"abortion storms"},{"label":"B","text":"clinically inapparent respiratory disease"},{"label":"C","text":"severe central nervous system disease"},{"label":"D","text":"anemia"},{"label":"E","text":"mucopurulent ocular and nasal discharge"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-96dfbd8499f0","stem":"Decoquinate is continually fed to confined poultry in order to control","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leucocytozoon spp"},{"label":"B","text":"Isospora spp."},{"label":"C","text":"Ascaridia spp."},{"label":"D","text":"Trichomonas gallinae"},{"label":"E","text":"Eimeria spp.."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c1c4312ba979","stem":"Which tick of dogs is also referred to as the “kennel tick” because it can survive inside homes and kennels, creating an indoor source of infestation for dog","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhipicephalus sanguineus, the brown dog tick"},{"label":"B","text":"Amblyomma americanum, the lone star tick"},{"label":"C","text":"Dermacentor variabilis, the American dog tick"},{"label":"D","text":"Ixodes scapularis, the black-legged tick"},{"label":"E","text":"Amblyomma maculatum, the Gulf Coast tick"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-168faecd0845","stem":"The preferred method for detecting the larvae of Aelurostrongylus abstrusus in cats is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"fecal sedimentation"},{"label":"B","text":"fecal filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"Baermann examination of feces"},{"label":"D","text":"direct smear of feces"},{"label":"E","text":"thoracic radiographs Page 17 of 19"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6371da706cf3","stem":"You necropsy a turkey and find target-shaped circular areas of necrosis in the liver and caseous cores in the ceca. What is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascaridia gallinae"},{"label":"B","text":"Histomonas meleagridis"},{"label":"C","text":"Eimeria meleagridis"},{"label":"D","text":"Tyzzeria pernicoiosa"},{"label":"E","text":"Trichomonas gallinae"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca2e3af9c573","stem":"A 3-year-old spayed female outdoor cat presents with respiratory distress. Thoracic radiographs reveal 3 large, distinct pulmonary cysts. Operculate eggs are found on fecal sedimentation. The most appropriate treatment is","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"praziquantel"},{"label":"B","text":"thoracotomy to allow complete surgical excision of the cystic masses"},{"label":"C","text":"pyrantel"},{"label":"D","text":"ivermectin"},{"label":"E","text":"melarsomine dihydrochloride"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7f8aeef9877d","stem":"Several elk and moose in a mixed-species hoof stock exhibit at a wildlife park develop neurologic disease, including ataxia and torticollis. However, the white-tailed deer in the exhibit are all normal. You suspect a nematode may be responsible. If so, how did the animals become infected?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ingestion of snail or slug intermediate hosts infected with third-stage larvae (L3)"},{"label":"B","text":"ingestion of first-stage larvae (L1) shed in feces of infected deer"},{"label":"C","text":"ingestion of third-stage larvae (L3) free on pasture"},{"label":"D","text":"skin penetration by first-stage larvae (L1) shed in feces of infected deer"},{"label":"E","text":"skin penetration of third-stage larvae (L3) free on pasture"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d1d9014aa4f","stem":"You examine a herd of goats with pale mucous membranes and submandibular edema. Necropsy examination of one goat reveals nematodes in the abomasum with a characteristic spirally-striped, “barber pole” appearance. What parasite is this most likely to be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teladorsagia circumcincta"},{"label":"B","text":"Trichostrongylus colubriformis"},{"label":"C","text":"Haemonchus contortus"},{"label":"D","text":"Nematodirus battus"},{"label":"E","text":"Cooperia punctata"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-33c95890022d","stem":"Although variability exists between animals, lesions of flea-bite dermatitis in cats most commonly develop on the","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"dorsum at the base of the tail"},{"label":"B","text":"neck"},{"label":"C","text":"pinna of the ears"},{"label":"D","text":"ventral pelvic area"},{"label":"E","text":"lateral elbows and lateral hocks"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9efafc2e9a51","stem":"Examination of a direct smear from fresh diarrheic feces of a kitten revealed several motile nematode larvae, each ~ 300 microns long, with a distinct rhabditiform esophagus. The most appropriate treatment for this parasite is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"metronidazole"},{"label":"B","text":"fenbendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"praziquantel"},{"label":"D","text":"piperazine"},{"label":"E","text":"epsiprantel Page 18 of 19 Key to the CAPC Parasitology Board Review Questions"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-bf91e4cc5d47","stem":"2ND CAT – 2005/2006 B. Sc [Nursing], MD II, BSc. (Laboratory Sciences) Time: 2 hours SECTION 1 – MSQ One of the following mosquito species is strongly, exophilic Anopheles arabiensis Aedes aegypti Culex quinquefasciatus An. funestus The most important environmental physical factor in the transmission of filariasis is ............................................................................................... Two of the following clinical symptoms are typical for American trypanasomiasis (Chagas disease). Calaabar swelling Sign of Romana Chyluria Chagoma Two of the following anti malarial drugs have a plant origin:- Halofantrine & Primaquine Quinine and Pyrimethamine Artemisinin and Quinidine Artemether and Chloroquine What are the four antimalarials that have been recommended in the current “Guide for Malaria Diagnosis and Treatment in Tanzania – 2005”?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antimalarial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breeding Conditions"},{"label":"B","text":"Aedes aegypti"},{"label":"C","text":"Polluted water"},{"label":"D","text":"An. gambiae"},{"label":"E","text":"Water in small containers and plant axils"},{"label":"F","text":"Cx. quniquefascitus"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e0d85aac2a9a","stem":"This is not a defense system of GIT?\\par","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"\\pard{\\pntext\\f0 a.\\tab}{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlbody\\pnf0\\pnindent360\\pnstart1\\pnlcltr{\\pntxta.}}"},{"label":"B","text":"\\fi-360\\li720\\tx720\\cf1 IgG\\par"},{"label":"C","text":"\\pard{\\pntext\\f0 b.\\tab}{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlbody\\pnf0\\pnindent360\\pnstart2\\pnlcltr{\\pntxta.}}"},{"label":"D","text":"\\fi-360\\li720\\tx720\\cf0 Mucous\\par"},{"label":"E","text":"\\pard{\\pntext\\f0 c.\\tab}{\\*\\pn\\pnlvlbody\\pnf0\\pnindent360\\pnstart3\\pnlcltr{\\pntxta.}}"},{"label":"F","text":"\\fi-360\\li720\\tx720 Gastric acid\\par"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7587b549ac1","stem":"Regarding immunogenicity of an antigen which one is wrong?\\par","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"its foreignness\\par \\cf1 b. its structural simplicity\\par \\cf0 c. the genetic constitution of the host\\par"},{"label":"D","text":"its epitopes\\par \\par"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-483e42611a33","stem":"Which is false regarding IgA\\par \\cf1 a. activates classical pathway of compliment system\\par \\cf2 b. found in saliva and tears\\par","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"confer immunity on mucosal surfaces\\par"},{"label":"D","text":"at the site of action it appears as a dimmer\\par \\par"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0742729cabb7","stem":"which of the following is incorrect regarding cell mediated response? \\par \\cf1 a. evolves within 24 hours\\par \\cf2 b. is important in controlling skin fungal disease\\par","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"is influenced by local chemotactic factor\\par"},{"label":"D","text":"treatment with steroids diminishes it.\\par \\par"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b48080f8995","stem":"Which of the following plays role in non specific local immunity?\\par \\cf0\\b a. nasal cells\\par \\b0 b. phagocytes\\par","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"macrophages\\par"},{"label":"D","text":"interferons\\par \\par"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bdc84433ac68","stem":"Two of the following diseases are zoonotic: Giardiasis and visceral leishmaniasis Visceral leishmaniasis and amoebiasis Giardiasis and amoebiasis Visceral leishmaniasis and toxoplasmosis None of the above 2. Phlebotomine sandflies are responsible for the transmission of one of the following parasites: a) Plasmodia spp b)Leishmania spp c) Loa loa d) Trypanosoma spp e) Onchocerca spp 3. Lutzomyia spp are responsible for the transmission of one of the following parasites: a) Trypanosome cruzi b) Entamoeba histolytica c) Onchocerca volvus d) Leishmania spp e) Plasmodium spp 4.Visceral leishmaniasis is also known as : a) Espundia b) Kala- azar c) Oriental sore d) chiclero ulcer e) None of the above 5. Leishmaniasis is caused by a) Protozoa parasites of the genus Leishmania which have an intracellular (Promastigote) stage in the mammals and extracellular flagellate (amastigote) stage in sand flies. b) Protozoa parasites of the genus Leishmania which have an intracellular (amastigote) stage in the mammals and extracellular flagellate (Promastigote) stage in sand flies c) Trypanososomes of the T. brucei group. d) Infection with slender elongated worms which are called filarial worms e) None of the above 6) Which of the following parasites are classified in the class protozoa a) Leishmania donovani b) Leishmania aethiopica c) Loa loa d) a and c e) a and b 7) The amastigotes stage of leishmaniasis reside in the: a) Eosinophils b) Lymphocytes c) Lungs d) Macrophages e) None of the above 8. Which of the following is the vector of Leishmaniasis a) Glossina spp b) Culex spp c) Sandflies d) Bulinus spp e) None of the above 9. Cutaneous leishmaniasis is also known as : a) Espundia b) Kala- azar c) Oriental sore d) chiclero ulcer e) None of the above 10.Which of the following are the vectors of new world Leishmaniasis a) Phlebotomus spp b) Lutzomyia spp and c)Glossina spp d) Phlebotomus spp and Lutzomyia spp e) None of the above 11. One of the following parasites do not belong in the family of visceral leishmania complex. a) Leishmania donovani b) Leishmania chagasi c) Leishmania infantum d) Leishmania aethiopica e) Leishmania l. donovani 12. The predominant form of Leishmania donovani in its mammalian host is: a) Epimastigotes b) Merozoites c) Amastigotes d) Promastigotes e) Metacyclics 13. In which of the following diseases is infection diagnosed by identification of the intracellular organisms in a borne marrow aspirates ? a. Visceral larva migrans b. African Trypanosomiasis c.Plasmodium falciparum d. Plasmodium malariae e. Visceral leishmaniasis 14. One of the following parasites do not belong in the family of mucocutaneous leishmania complex. a) Leishmania braziliensis b) Leishmania amazonensii c) Leishmania mexicana d) Leishmania braziliensis comples e) Leishmania chagasi 15. Leishmania amastigotes evade the mammalian immune system by ______ a) becoming intracellular b) penetrating into privileged sites in the body c) the process of antigenic vaiation d) masking with host antigen e) causing polyclonal stimulation 16. The infectious agents causing _______ are transmitted by blood feeding sandflies a) Cutenous leishmaniasis b) filariasis c) African Trypanosomiasis d) Plasmodium malaria e) None of the above 17. One of the following parasites do not belong in the family of cutaneous leishmania complex. a) Leishmania tropica b) Leishmania chagasi c) Leishmania major d) Leishmania aethiopica e) None of the above 18.Transmission of new world cutaneous leishmaniasis is transmitted by a. bites of the genus Lutzomyia b. bites of the genus Phlebotomus c.bites of the genus Plasmodium d. bites of the genus leishmania e.None of the above 19.Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis is also known as : a) Espundia b) Kala- azar c) Oriental sore d) chiclero ulcer e) None of the above 20.Which of the following are the vectors of old world Leishmaniasis a) Phlebotomus spp b) Lutzomyia spp and c)Glossina spp d) Phlebotomus spp and Lutzomyia spp e) None of the above 21.One of the following Two hosts is not involved in the epidemiology of malaria: Plasmodium spp Bovine animal reservoir Human host Anopheline mosquito Blackflies 22. The male form of Plasmodium falciparum is a: a) Merozoite b) Oocyst c) Sporozoite d) Microgametocyte e) Macrogametocyte 23. Formation of crescent – shaped forms is a feature of the following parasite: a) Plasmodium falciparum b) P.vivax c) P.malariae d) P. ovale e) P.knowlesi 24. Concerning malaria parasites, only one of the following statements is CORRECT: a) They are extracellular b) They are phagocytic c) They are multicellular d) They do not require a vector e) They are intracellular 25. Concerning Plasmodium falciparum, only one of the following statements is NOT true: a) It causes malignant malaria b) Malaria kills fast if not treated c) It shrinks the size of the erythrocyte d) The end result of haemoglobulin digestion is pigment e) It forms the so – called crescents 26. About Plasmoudium falciparum, one of the follo Sleeping sickness ___________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________ Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis is also known as (1 mark) _____________________________ 3. The mode of transmission of Leishmaniasis is mainly through (3 marks) a)_____________________________________________________________ Other rare methods are: a)______________________________________________________________ b) _____________________________________________________________ 4. Write short notes on the following: (6 marks) a) Amastigotes ________________________________________________________________ __________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________ b) Promastigotes ____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ c) Leishmaniasis recidiva___________________________________________________ _______________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ 6. Name three syndromes caused by leishmania parasites and the main differences among them (6marks) ________________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________________________ 7. Which four parasite forms are found in the life cycle of Trypanosoma brucei? 8.Name 4 differences between T. b. rhodesiense and T. brucei gambiense a. ____________________________________________________________________ b._____________________________________________________________________ c._____________________________________________________________________ d._____________________________________________________________________ the life cycle of Plasmodium falciparum, the following stages are found in the liver cells: a)____________________________________________ b) ___________________________________________ c) ____________________________________________ And the following in the blood: a)_____________________________________________ b) _____________________________________________ c)______________________________________________ And the following in the Mosquito gut: a)_______________________________________________ b) ______________________________________________ c) ______________________________________________ 10) In the life cycle of Trypanosoma brucei infective parasite forms injected into human body by tsetse are called _____________________________________ b) These transform into trypomastigotes with three forms c) Of these forms, which one causes infection in human host? _______________________________________________________ d) Multiplication parasite stages in the invertebrate host are(2)_________________ Infective parasite stage is ______________________________________________ 5.What are the two trypanosome groups? (a)_____________________________ b_____________________________________ 14. Draw and label the life cycle of leishmaniasis (10 marks) ADITIONAL QUESTIONS MD II Name three tsetse fly groups and associated diseases Group_______________diseae__________________________ Group ______________disease___________________________ Group _______________disease__________________________ Glossine (Tsetse) species are attracted to feeding on a host by the following a)______________________________________ b) ______________________________________ c) ______________________________________ Five control methods against tsetse flies include:- a)________________________________________________ b)________________________________________________ c)________________________________________________ d) _______________________________________________ e) ________________________________________________ Simple or complete metamorphosis is common with____________ Trypanosomiasis vector. Clinical features of American trypanosomiasis for acute phase are ________________________and _________________________ ADDITIONAL QUESTIONS BSc (Nursing and BSc Lab) What are the 2 most proposed mechanism of protection against severe malaria in children under 6 months of age? a)______________________________________________ b) _____________________________________________ What happens to the immunity against malaria when an adult immune individual from a malaria endemic country (Tanzania) moves to North America for 10 years? _________________________________________________________________ Will this person still resist malaria on coming back?.____________________________________________________________ Why is it that exposure to malaria infection during the first 5 years of life Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis is also known as .......................... 5. The mode of transmission of Leishmaniasis is mainly through a).................................................................... Other rare methods are: a)....................................................................... b) .................................................................... 6. Write short notes giving examples on the following: a) Zoonosis ........................................................................................... ............................................................................................ ........................................................................................... ............................................................................................ b) Reservoir host. ..........................................","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intermediate host Hepnozoites"},{"label":"B","text":"P. vivax Anthropozoonosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Triatomid bug Schizogony"},{"label":"D","text":"T.b, gambiense T. cruzi"},{"label":"E","text":"T.b.rhodesiense Match the protozoan species with the correct order of developmental stages which most closely cor ÷ë÷äÜäÑÅÑÅÑÅÑÅÑ¶ä®ä®ä®ä®ä®ä®äÑä®ä®ä®äÑÅÑÅÑä®äÑÅÑÅÑä®äÑä®ä®ä®ä®ä®äÑÅÑ óèóèóèóèáèáèóèóèóèóáÙáÏáèóèóèóèóáÙáÙáÙáÙáÙáÙáÙáÙáÙáÈ3⁄4È ́È ́È©ÈžÈ ÷ð÷ðæð÷ð÷ð÷ðæð÷ðæðæðæðæðÞð×ðÌÀμ©μ©ðÞðŸðæðÞðÞðÞðÌÀÌð”ˆ ÷ðæÜðÜðÔðÔðÔð÷ðÔðÌÄ1⁄4Ä1⁄4Ä±¥Ìž–ž–žÌžÌžÌžÌžžÌžÌÔðÔðÔð...ð"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Psychiatry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b9fc1ed59d60","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"7D OTf‚;ÿ"},{"label":"C","text":"Ö"},{"label":"D","text":"YUtú`wÿ"},{"label":"E","text":"/e"},{"label":"F","text":"\\ŠCÿ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-66757cb1f4c0","stem":"Check pH level of blood: do you know the pH of blood?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c0353576d10f","stem":"Malaria anemia An elementary knowledge of hematology is recommended WHAT IS ANEMIA IN THE FIRST PLACE? HOW MANY TYPES OF ANEMIA DO YOU KNOW? Causes of severe malaria anemia In Pf malaria anemia is caused by 3 factors","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4918f2ab2ac0","stem":"Inhibition of erythropoietin (EPO) production. EPO is a cytokine produced primarily by the kidney and functions in production of RBCs. Therefore kidney failures leads to malaria anemia Marker of anemia • What is the etiology of the anemia?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b053d25e5f98","stem":"The adult stage of cestodes in the genus Taenia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOri