ing hormones except which one of the following hormones?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Head and Neck","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-748eb18e127a","stem":"The clinician tells the patient, \"The pathologist called your biopsy pre- cancerous\". That means A. a mass without anaplasia B. hyperplasia C. hypertrophy D. metaplasia E. dysplasia 29. What does TNM stands for? Tumor size, Lymph node, Malignancy Tumor size, Normal tissue, Metastasis Tumor size, Lymph node, Metastasis Tumor size, Nodular mass, Malignancy All of the above 30. Carcinoma in situ means? carcinoma of unknown origin occult carcinoma carcinoma with bad prognosis non invasive carcinoma Metastatic carcinoma","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dd6f9b03df2e","stem":"Correlations of both .................and .................data have lead to criteria for diagnosing tumors as either benign or malignant Clinical and statistical Histological and Clinical Histological and statistical Demographical and histological Sociological and economical 32. Accumulation of fluid in the pericardial cavity is referred to as which of the following terms? a. Pericarditis b. Pericardial Effusion c. Tamponade d. pleural effusion e. ascites 33. Which of the following terms refers to to rapid accumulation of fluid in the pericardial cavity, causing cardiac compression a. Pleural effusion b. Pericardial Effusion c. Tamponede d. All of the above 34. The pictured below shows a pathological heart particularly on the left ventricle. The pathological changes depicted is Hyperplasia Hypertension Hypertrophy Dysplasia Answer C","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-15b2507371f1","stem":"The image below shows aorta with a lesion. This is termed as? a. aortic aneurysm b. aortic dissection c. embolus d. hematoma","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleani