thology of perception, thinking and speech in clinical practice","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d4cde16745a6","stem":"Describe psychopathology within the family","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1ec28322f771","stem":"Describe the psychopathology of aging and the elderly Course status: CORE Course credits: 5.0 Total hours spent: 50 Organization of the Course Topic Title Lecture (Hrs) Tutorial/ Seminar (Hrs) Assignment (Hrs) Ind. Study (Hrs) Practical (Hrs) Total (Hrs) Credits 1. Introduction to psychopathology 10 4 2 4 - 20","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b3009e5afe3f","stem":"...A......Rose Waaler test P) Drug induced toxicity Type I-IV Cholecystitis, a complication of gall stones is; Inflammation of the liver Inflammation of the gall bladder Inflammation of the intestines Inflammation of the pancreas What are the types of gallstones? Red and green stones Calcium and uric acid stone Pink and green stone Cholesterol and pigment stone Which of the following is NOT true of the parasympathetic control of the heart?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It affects muscarinic receptors."},{"label":"B","text":"It decreases heart rate via the SA node."},{"label":"C","text":"It decreases conduction velocity via the AV node."},{"label":"D","text":"It can be blocked by beta blockers, e.g. propranolol Allergy is an exaggerated immune response that is manifested by what reaction? A) Immunopathology B) Hypersensitivity C) Inflammation D) Fever E) Autoimmunity Regarding anaphylactic (type I) and immune complex (type III) hypersensitivities, which of the following is the MOST accurate? A. IgE is involved in both anaphylactic and immune complex hypersensitivibes B. Complement is involved in both anaphylactic and immune complex hypersensitivities C. Less antigen is typically needed to trigger and anaphylactic reaction than an immune complex reaction D. Neutrophils play more important role in anaphylactic reactions than in immune complex reactions 6.Which is a risk factor for gallstones? Obesity Sudden loss of weighty Both of the above None of the above 7. What is injected into patients undergoing allergic desensitization? A) IgG antibodies B) IgE antibodies C) Monoclonal antibodies against IgE D) Corticosteroids E) Pure allergen to which the patient is allergic"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e3266bb7f614","stem":"C. Pruritis A 55-year-old woman with chronic atrial fibrillation suddenly developed an acute abdomen and was rushed to the emergency department. At emergency laparoscopy most of the small-bowel loops were dusky to purple-red in colour. Her mesenteric veins were patent. The most probable underlying pathological process is: A .Coagulative necrosis B .Dry gangrene C .Gas gangrene D .Liquefactive gangrene E .Wet gangrene SECTION II Alopecia is the absence of hair?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"B. False Atrophic skin is thin, translucent and wrinkled with easily visable blood vessels"},{"label":"B","text":"B. False A pustule is a visible collection of pus in a blister. Pustules can be seen in psoriasis"},{"label":"C","text":"B. False Scabies is a contagious skin condition caused by parasitic mites."},{"label":"D","text":"B. False Circumscribed lesion that is raised and consists of edema is urticaria"},{"label":"E","text":"COLUMN B"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-49b7fddd21ae","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"Qpÿ%‚@Wÿ"},{"label":"C","text":"Åg.DK*\\ÿ"},{"label":"D","text":"œ01⁄4ßf×ÿ"},{"label":"E","text":"ä À6„CZóÿ"},{"label":"F","text":"CE9*h\\šÿ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-76e561d2f7f8","stem":"Tuberculosis is transmitted through","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Respiratory Medicine","topicOriginal":"Respiratory Medicine","subtopic":"Tuberculosis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infected water"},{"label":"B","text":"Infected hands"},{"label":"C","text":"Infected blood"},{"label":"D","text":"Infected air"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Respiratory Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-def77fdccd61","stem":"Tubercular Granulomas are made up of: Genital ulcers:True a. may be due to herpes simplex virusTrue b. are associated with an increased incidence of HIVTrue c. if painful and associated with lymphadenopathy, are likely to be due to chancroidFalse d are found in gonorrhoeaTrue e. if well-defined and beefy red, are likely to be due to granuloma inguinale","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"organized aggregates of immune cells that surround foci of infected tissues"},{"label":"B","text":"Damaged tissue cells and bacilli only"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous and damaged tissue and bacilli only"},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotic tissue and damaged tissue and bacilli only Identify the letter of the choice that best completes the statement or answers the question. (2-points each) __d 1. Once sperm are produced by the testes they move into and mature in the a. seminiferous tubes c. vas deferens b. Penis d. Epididymis ___d_ 2. The purpose of the urethra is to a. carry only urine b. carry only semen c. carry a mixture of semen and urine d. carry both semen and urine but not at the same time ___c_ 3. The menstrual cycle is triggered each month by a. the release of blood from the uterus b. the buildup of the lining of the uterus c. Hormones d. sexual intercourse ____d 4. Female infertility can be caused by hormonal problems, blocked fallopian tubes, and a. a cold c. irregular menstrual cycles b. lack of appetite d. STDs ___a_ 5. Sexual feelings are normal and necessary a. for reproduction to occur b. for the heart and lungs to function properly c. for an ovum to be released from a female’s ovary each month d. for teens to become adults. __b__ 6. When sexual feelings occur, it is important to a. consciously choose to act responsibly b. go to a quiet place and talk about what you are feeling with your partner c. explore the feelings and do what feels best d. see a doctor ___c_ 7. The external male reproductive organs consist of the penis, testes, and a. Urethra c. Scrotum b. Prostate d. seminal visicle _c___ 8. The external female reproductive organs include a. the vagina, the clitoris, and the mons pubis c. the vaginal opening, the labia majora, and the clitoris b. the hymen, the vaginal opening, and the uterus d. the hymen, the ovaries, and the clitoris ___d_ 9. Semen leaves the male body when the penis is erect and a. urination occurs c. excitement occurs b. evacuation occurs d. ejaculation occurs ___c_ 10. The menstrual cycle is a result of a. the female body preparing to give birth c. the female body preparing for pregnancy b. the female body preparing to produce milk d. the female body preparing for old age True/False Indicate whether the sentence or statement is true or false. Answer “T” if you think the statement is true and “F” if you think the statement is false (2 points each) ___T_ 11. In order for pregnancy to occur, a male sperm and female ovum must unite. ___F_ 12. Ova are produced by the fallopian tubes in the female body. __F__ 13. The male sex hormone is estrogen. ___T_ 14. Removing the foreskin that covers the penis at birth is called circumcision. ___F_ 15. Coulper’s and the prostate are two kinds of hormones in the male body. ___T_ 16. It is easier to make responsible decisions about sexual feelings when you are alone with your boyfriend or girlfriend. ___F_ 17. Menstrual flow and urine exit the female body through the same opening. ___T_ 18. It is normal for males to ejaculate while they are sleeping. ___T_ 19. PMS is a condition that affects some females during pregnancy. ___T_ 20. An erect penis cannot return to its relaxed state without ejaculation. ___T_ 21. It is not always possible to control feelings of sexual arousal. ___T_ 22. The body’s first response to sexual stimulation includes increased heart rate, flushing, and faster breathing. Matching (1 mark each) Match each item with the correct statement below. . a. Scrotum e. Cervix b. Sterility f. Erection c. Vulva g. menstruation d. Uterus h. Penis ___b_ 23. inability to produce offspring ___a_ 24. a loose sac of skin that holds the testes ___d_ 25. a hollow muscular organ that receives and holds a fertilized ovum ___e_ 26. the opening to the uterus __h__ 27. a male organ that is used by males both for sexual reproduction and the elimination of body wastes ___c_ 28. the external female reproductive organs ___g_ 29. the process of shedding the lining of the uterus ___f_ 30. when the sponge-like tissues of the penis fill with blood and the penis becomes enlarged and hard TRUE/FALSE QUESTIONS (0.5 mark) each and NO PENALTY Consumption of raw fish or shellfish is associated with infection caused by True a. Clonorchis sinensisFalse b. Ancylostoma duodenaleFalse c. Schistosoma japonicumTrue d. Vibrio parahaemolyticusFalse e. Paragonimus westermani The following are recognised causes of eosinophilia False a. malariaFalse b. visceral leishmaniasisTrue c. Churg-Strauss syndrome True d. drug hypersensitivity True e. visceral larva migrans (toxocariasis) Characteristic features of kwashiorkor include:False a. patient aged less than 1 yearTrue b. anorexiaTrue c. flakey discoloured skinTrue d. hepatomegalyFasle e. splenomegaly ANSWERS TO Q14-20 Answers and Explanations"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7ecaf42d61da","stem":"A 54-year-old previously healthy woman is hospitalized for pneumonia. On the 10th hospital day she is found to have swelling and tenderness of her right leg, which has developed over the past 48 hours. Raising the leg elicits pain. An ultrasound examination reveals findings suggestive of femoral vein thrombosis. Which of the following conditions is most likely to have contributed the most to the appearance of these findings?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1894481425f5","stem":"A 66-year-old man with Parkinson disease develops pleuritic chest pain. On exam, he has guarding over the right lower lung with dullness to percussion. A chest CT scan shows a focal, wedge-shaped pleural based area of hemorrhage in the right lower lobe. Which of the following is the most likely cause for his pulmonary lesion?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5fc6bb6724a2","stem":"A 29-year-old woman is involved in a motor vehicle accident that results in severe lacerations to her lower extremities, along with blunt abdominal trauma. In the emergency room she is noted to have cool, pallid skin. She has vital signs showing T 36.9°C, P 110/minute, RR 26/minute, and BP 70/30 mm Hg. She has decreased urine output. Which of the following laboratory findings on a blood sample from this patient is most likely to be present?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2280a0e79d25","stem":"A 68-year-old woman has survived multiple episodes of pulmonary thromboembolism during the past three months. On physical examination there are no abnormal findings. Which of the following is the most likely underlying condition leading to this patient's recurrent pulmonary thromboembolism?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c0478b4bdac2","stem":"A 30-year-old man goes to his physician for a routine health checkup. On physical examination there are no abnormal findings. Laboratory test findings include serum glucose 80 mg/dL, hemoglobin A1C 4%, total cholesterol 240 mg/dL, LDL cholesterol 180 mg/dL, and HDL cholesterol 20 mg/dL. Through which of the following mechanisms is endothelial vascular injury in this patient most likely to occur?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-65bbf2c64112","stem":"What type of cell does mitosis create?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b711c8194c13","stem":"What is the correct statement to describe the difference between a sex cell and a somatic cell?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d5a6ad5fb49b","stem":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about genes?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90ad377ba64d","stem":"If an organism is DdGg, what is one of the alleles that would be found in the gametes produced?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d6f0722425b","stem":"Who is considered the father of genetics?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Genetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Genetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f7d1825e19c8","stem":"If you wanted to determine the phenotype of an organism, what procedure would you follow?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81e24cfa7353","stem":"If a disease is said to be sex-linked, what pair of chromosomes must contain the gene responsible for the disease?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Genetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Genetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-650e654371b4","stem":"If an organism is trisomy 21, how many total chromosomes do they have?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Genetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Genetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7b08d9dd0241","stem":"During anaphase of Meiosis, the chromosomes fail to separate properly. This causes there to be an uneven amount distributed to the resulting daughter cells. What is this called?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Genetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Genetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7368feed9873","stem":"A 27-year-old man comes to the physician for an infertility work-up. He and his wife have been trying to conceive a child for 6 years. Physical examination shows bilateral gynecomastia, reduced testicular size, reduced body hair, and increased length between the soles of his feet and the pubic bone. A semen analysis indicates oligospermia. Laboratory studies show increased follicle-stimulating hormone level and slightly decreased testosterone level. Which of the following karyotypes is most likely to be present in this man?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d2e8f426050","stem":"A 25-year-old woman with amenorrhea has never had menarche. On physical examination, she is 145 cm (4 ft 9 in) tall. She has a webbed neck, a broad chest, and widely spaced nipples. Strong pulses are palpable in the upper extremities, but there are only weak pulses in the lower extremities. On abdominal MR imaging, the ovaries are small, elongated, and tubular. Which of the following karyotypes is most likely to be present in this patient?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c25cac7bd2f7","stem":"A 10-year-old boy who is mentally retarded is able to carry out activities of daily living, including feeding and dressing himself. On physical examination, he has brachycephaly and oblique palpebral fissures with prominent epicanthal folds. A transverse crease is seen on the palm of each hand. On auscultation of the chest, there is a grade III/VI systolic murmur. Which of the following diseases is he most likely to develop by age 20?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6eff9f3cea7f","stem":"A 39-year-old woman gives birth to a term infant with a right transverse palmar crease, low-set ears, oblique palpebral fissures, and a heart murmur. The infant survives to childhood and exhibits only mild mental retardation. Which of the following chromosomal abnormalities is most likely to be present in the somatic cells of this child?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d495ae44db5b","stem":"A 13-year-old boy has been drinking large quantities of fluids and has an insatiable appetite. He is losing weight and has become more tired and listless for the past month. Laboratory findings include normal CBC and fasting serum glucose of 175 mg/dL. Which of the following is the probable inheritance pattern of this disease?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Genetics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Genetics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a12b11243739","stem":"Has no fixed pattern and occurs in piece-meal fashion?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-565903e2264d","stem":"What's the characteristic cell in tissue inflammation caused by worms?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ddf7464dfbbb","stem":"Which of the following statements best defines the term “labile cells”?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e71211980b74e761dcf049d9e9a4f421fa7b9b9d"},{"id":"q-50fe42513725","stem":"A 35-year-old man asks for advice regarding seasonal eye itching and runny nose. Recurrent conjunctivitis in this patient is most likely caused by which of the following mechanisms of disease?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"17d5ac8097482d30c7444cfb93c5474bbf671643"},{"id":"q-634c447ff24d","stem":"A 52-year-old woman with a history of systemic hypertension and chronic renal failure undergoes kidney transplantation, but the graft fails to produce urine. A renal biopsy is diag- nosed as “hyperacute transplant rejection.” Graft rejection in this patient is caused primarily by which of the following mediators of immunity and inflammation?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dcec35ff0300","stem":"A 20-year-old woman with a history of asthma and aller- gies undergoes skin testing to identify potential allergens in her environment. A positive skin reaction to ragweed in this patient would be mediated by which of the following classes of immunoglobulin?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Respiratory Medicine","topicOriginal":"Respiratory Medicine","subtopic":"Asthma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Respiratory Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d7b0c187d688","stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a 1-year history of dry mouth and eyes. A biopsy of a minor salivary gland reveals infiltrates of lymphocytes forming focal germinal centers. Which of the following cellular organelles is a target for autoantibodies in this patient?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-caf39da98690","stem":"Which of the following enzymes converts the HIV genome into double-stranded DNA in host cells.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-739834013ce8","stem":"A 50-year-old woman complains of intermittent tingling and pain in the tips of her fingers. She also reports joint and muscle pain. Physical examination reveals lymphadenopathy. Labora- tory studies show hypergammaglobulinemia. The antinuclear antibody test is positive, but there is no evidence of antibod- ies against double-stranded DNA. Urinalysis is normal. The patient responds well to steroids. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1897bdf12e83","stem":"A 20-year-old gardener presents to his family physician for treatment of what he describes as “poison ivy.” The patient’s hands and arms appear red and are covered with oozing blis- ters and crusts. Which of the following best describes the pathogenesis of these skin lesions?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f2a09bbdf71","stem":"A 40-year-old man complains of having yellow skin and sclerae, abdominal tenderness, and dark urine. Physical examination reveals jaundice and mild hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies demonstrate elevated serum bilirubin (3.1 mg/dL), decreased serum albumin (2.5 g/dL), and prolonged prothrombin time (17 seconds). Serologic tests reveal antibodies to hepatitis B core antigen (IgG anti-HBcAg). The serum is also positive for HBsAg and HBeAg. Which of the following glycoproteins serves as the principal cell surface receptor for viral antigens on B lymphocytes in this patient?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6042743f1b06","stem":"A 30-year-old woman is found to have a congenital immuno- deficiency that has remained largely asymptomatic throughout her life. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9e2d378959d8","stem":"A 28-year-old woman with a history of drug abuse presents with an infectious mononucleosis-like syndrome and lymph- adenopathy. Blood tests subsequently indicate that she is HIV-positive. Which of the following lymphocyte-associated proteins mediates the entry of HIV into host cells in this patient?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7af0f8a72b9a","stem":"A 4-year-old girl presents for a preschool physical examination. The child has a small head circumference, thin upper lip, and low-bridge nose. She shows evidence of mild mental retardation. Her parents state that she is often “emotional.” Which of the following maternal causes of birth defects most likely accounts for these clinicopathologic findings?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f03c0db51e28","stem":"A 16-year-old girl complains that she has not started menstruating like other girls her age. The patient is short (4 ft, 11 in) and has a thick-webbed neck. Physical examination reveals widely spaced nipples and poor breast development. If this patient’s genetic disease was caused by nondisjunction during mitosis of a somatic cell in the early stages of embryogenesis, which of the following is the patient’s most likely karyotype?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1149511b0bb2","stem":"The mother of a newborn boy is alarmed that her baby regurgitates at every feeding. An endoscopic examination reveals that the child’s esophagus is almost completely occluded. This finding represents an example of which of the following errors of morphogenesis?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-702d922cbced","stem":"A 27-year-old woman presents for a pregnancy test. She recalls drinking heavily during the week in which she may have conceived. What is the most likely consequence of toxic exposure to the conceptus during early (preimplantation) development?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c14a2cc4d70a","stem":"A 3-year-old boy dies in an automobile accident. At autopsy, the right lung is markedly shrunken. Dissection shows that the right main stem bronchus ends blindly in nondescript tissue composed of rudimentary ducts and connective tissue. This finding represents an example of which of the following errors of morphogenesis?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b2c158769f5","stem":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman delivers a baby prematurely at 28 weeks of gestation. Shortly after birth, the neonate becomes short of breath, with intercostal retraction and nasal flaring during respiration. The neonate is placed on a ventilator, but dies of respiratory insufficiency and intraventricular hemorrhage. Microscopic examination of the lungs at autopsy is shown. The eosinophilic material lining the air spaces represents an accumulation of which of the following proteins?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2991e0682e57","stem":"If the neonate described in Question 16 had survived, which of the following would be the most likely complication related to anoxia and acidosis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7fff2d80f265","stem":"A 28-year-old woman gives birth to a stillborn with a severe neural tube defect (neonate shown in the image). This birth defect was caused by an error of morphogenesis that occurred at which of the following stages of development after fertilization?","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Embryology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":